From: Trijal Saha <97483939+Trijal08@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] aims: build CALL_MANAGE, for hold, resume and conference

CALL_MANAGE (0x0d09) was named in the id table and nothing built it, so hold,
resume and merging into a conference had no wire at all.

Decoded from a capture of this device's own Android rather than from
ShannonIms: the capture was taken one pressed button at a time against a
baseline containing no 0x0d09 whatsoever, so every frame is attributable to
one action. Two payload bytes, the operation then the call it applies to:

    op 01 call 0   hold
    op 02 call 0   resume
    op 01 call 0   auto-hold when a second call was added
    op 01 call 1   hold the active second call...
    op 06 call ff  ...then merge, 0.38 s later
    op 01 call 2   hold the surviving call after the other leg hung up
    op 02 call 2   resume it

So op 1 is hold, op 2 resume, op 6 conference, and a conference is addressed
to call id 0xff -- every call -- rather than to a particular one.

**A merge is two frames.** The dialler holds the active call and only then
sends the conference; a bare op 6 never appears on the wire. That is written
on the builder because it is the kind of thing a caller gets wrong, and
reading the APK would not have revealed it.

The FFI rejects any other operation rather than passing it through, since the
remaining ones -- split, swap of two established calls, hangup-all, UDUB,
transfer -- are not captured yet and their numbering is unknown.

A test pins all four frames byte for byte against the capture.
diff -ruN a/crates/samsung-aims/src/call.rs b/crates/samsung-aims/src/call.rs
--- a/crates/samsung-aims/src/call.rs	2026-11-09 17:22:50.252444443 -0500
+++ b/crates/samsung-aims/src/call.rs	2026-11-09 17:23:09.874158804 -0500
@@ -140,6 +140,35 @@
     )
 }
 
+/// What `CALL_MANAGE` (0x0d09) is being asked to do, in the CP's numbering.
+///
+/// Decoded from a capture of this device's own Android, one pressed button at
+/// a time against a baseline with no `CALL_MANAGE` in it at all, so each value
+/// is attributable to one action rather than inferred from the APK.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum CallManageOp {
+    /// Put the named call on hold.
+    Hold = 1,
+    /// Take the named call off hold.
+    Resume = 2,
+    /// Merge everything into a multiparty call. Takes [`CALL_ID_ALL`].
+    Conference = 6,
+}
+
+/// The call id `Conference` is addressed to: every call, not a particular one.
+pub const CALL_ID_ALL: u8 = 0xff;
+
+/// `CALL_MANAGE` (0x0d09) -- hold, resume, or merge into a conference.
+///
+/// Two bytes: the operation, then the call it applies to.
+///
+/// **A merge is two frames, not one.** The captured dialler holds the active
+/// call and only then sends `Conference`, 0.38 s later; a bare `Conference`
+/// was never seen on the wire. Callers should reproduce the pair.
+pub fn build_call_manage(token: u16, op: CallManageOp, call_id: u8) -> Vec<u8> {
+    frame(msg_id::CALL_MANAGE, token, &[op as u8, call_id])
+}
+
 /// Which half of a key press `SEND_DTMF` carries, in the CP's own numbering.
 ///
 /// `ImsCall` picks between them: `sendDtmf` sends [`DtmfType::Burst`] and lets
@@ -341,6 +370,27 @@
 
 #[cfg(test)]
 mod tests {
+
+    /// The frames the captured Android dialler sent, byte for byte. Hold and
+    /// resume name a call; a conference is addressed to every call.
+    #[test]
+    fn call_manage_matches_the_capture() {
+        let hold = build_call_manage(0x0001, CallManageOp::Hold, 0);
+        assert_eq!(u16::from_le_bytes([hold[4], hold[5]]), 14);
+        assert_eq!(&hold[2..4], &msg_id::CALL_MANAGE.to_le_bytes());
+        assert_eq!(&hold[12..14], &[0x01, 0x00]);
+
+        let resume = build_call_manage(0x0002, CallManageOp::Resume, 0);
+        assert_eq!(&resume[12..14], &[0x02, 0x00]);
+
+        // The second leg was held by id before the merge.
+        let hold1 = build_call_manage(0x0003, CallManageOp::Hold, 1);
+        assert_eq!(&hold1[12..14], &[0x01, 0x01]);
+
+        let conf = build_call_manage(0x0004, CallManageOp::Conference, CALL_ID_ALL);
+        assert_eq!(&conf[12..14], &[0x06, 0xff]);
+    }
+
